    "result_text": "All 21 tests pass, including the new Cp12 (address-normalize) checkpoint and the updated Cp01, Cp10, Cp11.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization for owner creation, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions and the existing `OwnerTelephone`/`OwnerEmail` normalization pattern.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `OwnerAddress.java`: a shared helper that normalizes an address to its canonical form \u2014 trims, collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). It exposes both an in-place `normalize(OwnerFieldsDto)` and a pure `normalize(String)` (returning `\"\"` for null/whitespace-only).\n\n**`ValidateOwnerFields.java`**: normalizes the address in place before the required-field check. So the address stored/returned is canonical (`\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`), and an address that is blank after normalization is added to the missing-fields list and rejected as 400.\n\n**`OwnerHousehold.java`**: the household duplicate check (`sameHousehold`) and the shared household id (`id`) now compare addresses via `OwnerAddress.normalize(...)`, so casing/whitespace/abbreviation differences no longer defeat a match. (Last-name comparison keeps its existing case-insensitive normalization \u2014 abbreviation expansion is intentionally not applied to names.) I preserved the pre-existing NUL-byte separator in the id key untouched.\n\nBecause the normalized DTO flows through `BuildOwner` \u2192 `SaveOwner` \u2192 response and into the household steps unchanged, all four affected behaviours (storage/return, required-field rejection, duplicate detection, and household id) use the single normalized form.\n\nAll 21 acceptance tests pass, including the new `Cp12Tests` and the updated `Cp01`/`Cp10`/`Cp11`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: I noticed a stray NUL byte embedded in the household-id separator string literal in `OwnerHousehold.id` (`\"\\0\"` rather than `\" \"`). It's pre-existing and functionally harmless (a consistent separator), so I left it as-is \u2014 but let me know if you'd like it cleaned up to a normal space.",
